From: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw89: 8852c: add radar detection for the RTL8832BR

An AP on a DFS channel needs radar detection. rtw89 has none, so hostapd
fails to start its channel availability check and the RTL8832BR can't
use channels 52-64 and 100-144.

The baseband reports every pulse it sees: width, interval to the previous
pulse and a chirp flag. The MAC collects the reports and sends them to
the host as RX packets of type 8, in batches of up to 61 pulses or after
80 ms. Set this up with R_AX_DFS_CFG0 whenever the channel needs DFS; the
BB report block must be enabled first, which the MAC init leaves off.

Detect radars in software, following the detector of the vendor driver
for this chip (halbb, gen0 report format): each batch is matched against
the radar types of the DFS region (FCC, ETSI, or Japan W53/W56), and
long chirp radars are counted across batches. The tolerances follow the
traffic load as in the vendor driver. On a match, report the radar to
mac80211, which stops the CAC or makes hostapd switch channels.

Advertise radar detection for 20, 40 and 80 MHz for the RTL8832BR. A
debugfs file shows the statistics and can simulate a radar.

Tested on a Cudy WR1500 (country CA): CAC on channels 100 and 36-64
completes, and a radar near the test site (chirped 92 us pulses in
groups every 5.3 s, matching FCC type 5) is detected and makes hostapd
move to channel 36. In a simulation of the vendor test waveforms, FCC
types 0-5 and ETSI types 1-6 are detected in 99.6-100 % of the trials,
and random pulses (10-1000 per second, one hour) give no detection.

Signed-off-by: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
